>
>My Valks and Samurai were always human characters wheras my Wizard is
>an Elf - does class or race have anything to do with it, or was I just
>plain lucky before and not lucky with my Wiz.

I'd say you were just plain lucky (and wielding a better weapon) before.
(Or maybe wearing an amulet of magical breathing.)

There is never any good reason (and this has been said time and again)
to engage ';' in melee.  None of them resist magic missile or fingering,
nor will they resist the humble thrown rock (or being teleported away,
which will always land them on dry land).  Go smash a boulder and throw
rocks at the nasty horrid ';' and they won't trouble you a bit.

If a monster grabs you while you are not absolutely certain you and it
are on dry land, teleport yourself, or it, away immediately.  Break a
wand of teleportation or read a cursed scroll of teleportation (or use
the Eye or the Orb of Fate, if applicable) if you absolutely have to
(frex if you are confused on the turn that you get grabbed), but *make
sure that you and it are no longer adjacent to each other*.

A ray-type attack or a missile or melee attack might miss.  A teleport
beam won't, and neither will a teleport storm.

Greasing your outermost layer of armour also helps; if the outer layer
of (body armour, cloak) is greased, the grab attack will slip off it
(removing the grease in the process a certain percentage of the time).
An oilskin cloak achieves the same effect but never wears off.  An
amulet of magical breathing makes you immune to drowning (and also to
death by overeating, so I often use it as my default amulet if I've got
some other source of reflection).

These days I treat AoLS as inferior to AoR, AoMB, and AoESP (and for
early characters, inferior to AvP as well).

